<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tell Halaf</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Guzana, Gozan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tell Halaf is an archaeological site in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> al-Ayn on the Turkish border. It gave its name to the Halaf culture of the 6th millennium BCE, known for its painted pottery. In the early 1st millennium BCE it was Guzana, capital of the Aramaean kingdom of Bit-Bahiani and later an Assyrian province, the biblical Gozan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tell-halaf.jpg" alt="Tell Halaf" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TellHalaf,NE-palace1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tell Halaf lies in Al-Hasakah in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, a few kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> al-Ayn, in the fertile valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur River</a>. It was the first site excavated of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> culture later named for it, and it flourished from about 6100 to 5400 BCE. The Halaf culture was succeeded in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid culture</a>, and the site was then abandoned for a long period. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a> controlled the region from the 15th century BCE until about 1345 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> conquered Carchemish and Tell Halaf became a Hittite city under the viceroy of Carchemish.</p><p>In the 10th century BCE the rulers of the small Aramaean kingdom of Bit Bahiani took their seat at the site, refounded as Guzana or Gozan. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapara">Kapara</a> built the hilani, a palace in Neo-Hittite style decorated with statues and relief orthostats. In 894 BCE Adad-nirari II recorded it as a tributary Aramaean city-state, and in 808 BCE the city and its surroundings became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>, with the governor's seat in a palace on the eastern part of the citadel mound. Guzana survived the collapse of Assyria and remained inhabited until the Roman-Parthian period. The lower town extended 600 metres north to south and 1,000 metres east to west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>On 19 November 1899 the German diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-von-oppenheim">Max von Oppenheim</a>, travelling for Deutsche Bank on the route of the Bagdad Railway, found the site after local tales of stone idols buried in the sand. Several statues were uncovered within three days, among them a &quot;Sitting Goddess&quot;, and a test pit reached the entrance to the Western Palace. Having no permit, he reburied them. He began a full campaign on 5 August 1911 with five archaeologists and five hundred local workers, financed by his father's banking fortune, at a cost of around 750,000 marks. Locals had meanwhile damaged some finds, partly from superstition and partly to obtain building material.</p><p>The dig found the large statues and reliefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapara">Kapara</a>'s palace, a cult room and tombs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> pottery, then, with the finds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, the oldest painted pottery known. After the First World War Oppenheim sold some pieces to museums, returned to the site in 1927 and resumed digging in 1929. His finds were divided with the French authorities: about 80, two thirds, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and 35 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, forming the core of the National Museum of Aleppo. He opened a private Tell Halaf Museum in Berlin-Charlottenburg in July 1930. In November 1943 a British phosphorus bomb burned it completely; the basalt pieces were shattered by thermal shock when the fire was fought, and many statues burst into dozens of pieces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tell Halaf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aramaean tribes took over the upper Khabur in the 10th century BCE, and the House of Bahiani ruled from <b>Guzana</b>, at Tell Halaf on the Syrian-Turkish border.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-bahiani">Bit Bahiani</a></li></ul></section>
